OS_IOS, and the three places where Darwin is not the desktop.

The engine has no notion of iOS. Adding one as a *third* Apple platform would
mean auditing every OS_MACOSX site; adding it **in addition** to OS_MACOSX
means the opposite: everything that is true of Darwin stays true, and OS_IOS
marks only the differences. There turn out to be three, all of them frameworks
that exist on the desktop and not on the phone:

* `ApplicationServices` in lib/sysdep/os/osx/osx.cpp. Nothing in that file uses
  it -- the file only wants _NSGetExecutablePath and the bundle helpers -- so
  the include is a leftover, and on iOS there is no such framework.

* `AppKit` in osx_atlas.mm. Atlas is a wxWidgets editor in a second process,
  started through NSWorkspace; none of that exists on iOS. The function keeps
  its declaration so callers still link, and does nothing. It is never reached:
  --without-atlas is the only configuration that makes sense here.

* `FSEvents` in osx/dir_watch.cpp. iOS has CoreServices but not this part of
  it. Directory watching is a desktop convenience -- it makes an edited mod
  file show up without a restart -- so both entry points report success and do
  nothing. The only caller discards the result (`(void)dir_watch_Add(...)` in
  lib/file/common/real_directory.cpp) and never needs the watch pointer to be
  filled in.

What does *not* need touching is worth listing, because it is the reason this
patch is short: osx_paths.mm and osx_bundle.mm use only Foundation, which is
the same on both platforms, so NSSearchPathForDirectoriesInDomains keeps
returning the right directories -- inside the app container on iOS. And
osx_sys_version.mm compiles because AvailabilityMacros.h does not define
MAC_OS_X_VERSION_MIN_REQUIRED when the target is iOS, so it takes its own
fallback path; the value it then reports is only read by the dir_watch code
that iOS does not compile.

--- source/lib/sysdep/os.h
+++ source/lib/sysdep/os.h
@@ -66,6 +66,21 @@
 #else
 # define OS_MACOSX 0
 #endif
+// iOS and the iOS Simulator.
+// Deliberately *in addition* to OS_MACOSX rather than instead of it: iOS is
+// Darwin, and everything the engine does for Mach - the osx sysdep directory,
+// Foundation for the paths, the bundle layout - is right here too. OS_IOS
+// marks only the places where the desktop and the phone really differ.
+#if OS_MACOSX
+# include <TargetConditionals.h>
+# if defined(TARGET_OS_IPHONE) && TARGET_OS_IPHONE
+#  define OS_IOS 1
+# else
+#  define OS_IOS 0
+# endif
+#else
+# define OS_IOS 0
+#endif
 // BSD
 #if defined(__FreeBSD__) || defined(__NetBSD__) || defined(__OpenBSD__) || defined(__FreeBSD_kernel__)
 # define OS_BSD 1
--- source/lib/sysdep/os/osx/osx.cpp
+++ source/lib/sysdep/os/osx/osx.cpp
@@ -27,7 +27,11 @@
 #include "lib/utf8.h"
 #include "osx_bundle.h"
 
-#include <ApplicationServices/ApplicationServices.h>
+#if !OS_IOS
+// Nothing in this file uses ApplicationServices; the include is a leftover,
+// and on iOS the framework does not exist.
+# include <ApplicationServices/ApplicationServices.h>
+#endif
 #include <AvailabilityMacros.h> // MAC_OS_X_VERSION_MIN_REQUIRED
 #include <CoreFoundation/CoreFoundation.h>
 #include <mach-o/dyld.h> // _NSGetExecutablePath
--- source/lib/sysdep/os/osx/osx_atlas.mm
+++ source/lib/sysdep/os/osx/osx_atlas.mm
@@ -21,13 +21,29 @@
  */
 
